{"items":[{"recordId":"teach-n0000765","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"When an application assigns an argument, it gives that argument a level equal to the current level plus one.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false},{"role":"mechanism","basis":"source","text":"Thus, if the current level is n, the assigned argument level is n + 1; the assignment always moves one level upward.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000766","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The levels describe which sharing context an argument belongs to.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false},{"role":"limitation","basis":"source","text":"These levels do not describe the values or variables captured inside a runtime closure.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]}]}
